  "metadata": {
    "title": "Exact Diffusion Design for Maximally Collective Stable Turing Patterns in Binary-Complex Mass-Action Networks",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22729355",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-12",
    "description": "<p>For every n &ge; 4, this preprint constructs an n-species binary-complex classical mass-action topology for which, throughout every positive-equilibrium realization, every principal subsystem below order n &minus; 1 is Hurwitz while one order-(n &minus; 1) subsystem is unstable. A general principal-minor diffusion-ray theorem yields an exact network law: for any homogeneously stable realization, a stationary crossing occurs along a positive diagonal diffusion ray exactly when d_Z &gt; 8 h_Z &Sigma;_{j=2}^{n&minus;2} d_j/h_j. This gives the nonattained fixed-equilibrium contrast infimum, the unit-equilibrium value 8(n &minus; 3), and the topology-wide stationary trade-off &chi;_D &chi;_H &gt; 8(n &minus; 3).</p>\n<p>At unit equilibrium, an exact rational design undergoes a primary supercritical bifurcation with locally exponentially stable positive patterns in the fixed-integrated-mass class. A second exact family redistributes heterogeneity between diffusion and equilibrium concentrations so that both contrasts are &Theta;(&radic;n), matching the topology-specific stationary minimax lower bound in exponent. The proof uses explicit flux-cone design, exhaustive strongly connected block classification, conservation-compatible center-manifold reduction, and inspectable symbolic certificates.</p>\n<p>The networks are synthetic. The bifurcation, stability, and robustness conclusions are local for each fixed dimension, and the stationary diffusion law does not classify arbitrary wave instability. This is a submission-ready author preprint and has not undergone journal peer review.</p>\n<p>Version 1.0.11 includes the main preprint, mathematical supplement, self-contained TeX source, portable exact-verification repository, and a separate data archive. The exact companion software release is archived at <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22625980\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22625980</a>.</p>",
    "access_right": "open",
